\n\\lambda =1.5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f61237-fa49-5ed3-bcfa-d84836b597af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336403} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7b5402-436f-591d-9e7b-0aa027a4e5b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337281} {:answer/id #uuid "b041068d-df1a-5631-8adb-48e6700ef36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337615} {:answer/id #uuid "c24ee89c-b106-52d8-8a73-816b1c6d33b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337758} {:answer/id #uuid "c7170309-72bb-505d-90ac-c9bb6d7751d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b041068d-df1a-5631-8adb-48e6700ef36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337615} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05dbc8a-785a-5c65-86b3-dcf4802e7da9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338571}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a97697a-0ce5-5dbb-bf9f-1e792385b4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70187" :question/problem "![](images/24/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we begin with the vector $b,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =b+4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=b+4a.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e92f9d-c8dc-5823-afa8-eae130a1ba43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364882} {:answer/id #uuid "3de53034-0a04-5d26-b49f-128745f4ea0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364883} {:answer/id #uuid "f76caff6-871d-509a-b53c-70f664edb2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364884} {:answer/id #uuid "8993d132-186e-5f70-b539-0ecadbfbfd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364885} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886} :answer-field/id #uuid "af541ef0-9d16-554c-878d-707d965733bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364881}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49639a9a-7a73-58c8-8e2e-c3ff76e8e2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-120250" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} {:answer/id #uuid "633d97e3-8d84-57c9-8ac7-bdd2378d9d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420449} {:answer/id #uuid "c2051bdd-22cb-509c-8fb6-340e90eb858d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4325a-2b33-5c98-a020-248e21f996f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420459} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d19e05-07c5-5adc-9a1c-131ee586eacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f94aaa-a8cf-5aa4-9f93-884e35c10a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77230" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110336 :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763 :topic/math-academy-id 97 :topic/title "Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :knowledge-point/ques